Yes—the asymmetry you’re pointing at is real at the level of reproductive mechanics.

For women, orgasm and conception are much more separable processes. A woman can have intense sexual pleasure with no vaginal penetration at all, and conception generally requires sperm reaching the reproductive tract; female orgasm itself is not required. So there really is a built-in distinction between sexual pleasure and reproductive exposure.

Men have some separation too—nonpenetrative sex, masturbation, and even orgasm without ejaculation can occur—but ejaculation is much more directly tied to the reproductive payload. So the coupling is tighter.

Where I’d stop short is the next inference: that this arrangement was specifically designed so women in a harem could have sex with each other while preserving fidelity to one man. Anatomy supports that behavior as a possibility, but it doesn’t tell us that was the purpose.

The cleaner formulation is: female anatomy gives unusually strong decoupling between pleasure and reproduction, and humans can build social rules around that decoupling in many different ways.

That is a more interesting observation than the narrower moral theory, because it survives even if the cultural interpretation changes.